TRANSLATIONS documentation, last updated on 13 Sep 2026

Everything the server says to one client -- a numeric, a NOTICE from the
server, a reply from a service bot -- can be translated.  The English text
stays in the sources; a translation is a plain GNU PO file the server reads
by itself, one per language, and a client chooses its languages with the
LANGUAGE command.  The design is doc/proposals/005-traducciones-po.md.

There is no msgfmt step, no .mo file and no libintl: the .po is what is
installed and what is read, and a /REHASH picks up a corrected one.


FOR THE PERSON TRANSLATING
==========================

A catalog is <code>.po -- es.po, es-ar.po, pt.po -- written with Poedit,
Weblate, Crowdin, msgmerge or an editor.  The template to start from is
po/core.pot in the source tree (regenerated with `cmake --build build
--target pot` when xgettext is installed):

  msginit -l es -i po/core.pot -o po/es.po        # a new language
  msgmerge -U po/es.po po/core.pot                # after the sources moved

The rules the server checks when it loads the file, entry by entry:

  * A translation is a format for ircd_snprintf().  Its directives -- %s,
    %d, %C, %Tu, %:#C, %*s and the rest -- must be exactly those of the
    original, in the same order, byte for byte.  Only the text between
    them is yours.  %% may appear anywhere.

    This is stricter than gettext's own check: ircd_snprintf has no %1$s,
    so a translation cannot reorder its arguments.  Rewrite the sentence
    around the order instead.  (Almost no message has more than two.)

  * No line break in a translation.  An IRC message is one line; a second
    one would be a second command.

  * The file is UTF-8, and says so in its header (or says nothing).
    charset=ISO-8859-1 refuses the whole file.

  * A plural entry (msgid_plural) has exactly the number of forms the
    header's Plural-Forms declares, each meeting the rules above against
    msgid_plural.  A catalog whose header has no Plural-Forms has no
    plurals: its plural entries are refused and the original is used.

An entry that fails is dropped -- the English text is used for it -- with a
line in the log naming the file, the line and the reason; the rest of the
file loads.  A file that does not parse at all (an unclosed string, a bad
header) is refused whole, and if a previous version of that language was
loaded it stays in force.

Two things to know that gettext users might not expect:

  * Numerics carry a context: the numeric's code.  The 401 is

      msgctxt "401"
      msgid "%s :No such nick"
      msgstr "%s :No existe ese nick"

    so two numerics with the same English text can be translated apart, and
    you can see which one you are working on.  Everything else -- explicit
    formats, notices, bot replies -- has no context.

  * The leading ':' and the parameters before it are part of the IRC line,
    not of the sentence.  "%s :No such nick" is <nick> then the text;
    keep the "%s :" and translate what follows.

A `fuzzy` entry is ignored, exactly as msgfmt ignores it; an entry with an
empty msgstr is untranslated and the original is used; `#~` obsolete
entries are skipped.

Nothing checks the length.  A translation is usually longer than its
original, and a line is cut at 512 bytes as it always was; keep the long
numerics (030-032, 477, 561-566) in mind.

What is never translated, and is not in the template: the log, anything
sent to several clients at once (channel messages, WALLOPS, server
notices), anything that crosses a server link as text (KILL and QUIT
reasons, SQUIT, ERROR), and the server's own command line.


FOR THE PERSON RUNNING A SERVER
===============================

Where the catalogs live
-----------------------

  $DPATH/po/<code>.po                              the ircd ("core")
  $DPATH/modules/<type>/<name>/po/<code>.po        a module's own

The first directory is PO_PATH in config.h, set with -DIRCU_POPATH= at
configure time (default $DPATH/po); `cmake --install` puts po/*.po from
the source tree there.  A module's catalogs are resources of the module
and are installed beside its .so; a module built from a single file has
no directory of its own and no translations.

Every domain -- "core" and one per module -- looks up only its own
catalogs; a module's text is never found in the ircd's file, nor the
other way round.  Drop a new pt.po into $DPATH/po/, /REHASH, and pt is
available; nothing is compiled.

The code is the file name: BCP 47 restricted to [A-Za-z]{2,3}(-[A-Za-z0-9]
{1,8})*, at most fifteen characters, matched without regard to case.  A
file whose name is not a code is ignored with a warning.

"en" -- the language of the sources -- is always available and needs no
catalog.  An en.po is allowed, if you want to reword the English.

How a client's language is chosen
---------------------------------

For each message, in order:

  1. each code of the client's LANGUAGE preference, and for each one the
     exact catalog and then its primary subtag (es-ar -> es-ar, es);
  2. DEFAULT_LANGUAGE, the same way;
  3. the original text.

"en" anywhere in that chain stops it: a client that asks for "en es" gets
English even where the server's default is Spanish.

  Features {
    "DEFAULT_LANGUAGE" = "es";      # what a client gets that asked for
                                    # nothing; empty (the default) means
                                    # the original text.  /SET works.
    "CAP_LANGUAGES" = "TRUE";       # advertise draft/languages
  };

DEFAULT_LANGUAGE is per server, so irc.es.example.net can speak Spanish
and irc.us.example.net English from the same image and the same po/.

The negotiation
---------------

The IRCv3 draft "draft/languages" as Ergo implements it.  CAP LS 302 shows
the languages available:

  [C] CAP LS 302
  [S] :irc.example.net CAP * LS :... draft/languages=3,es,en,pt

  <max>,<default>,<the rest alphabetically> -- 3 is the most codes a client
  may list, and the first code is DEFAULT_LANGUAGE when it is one of them.

  [C] LANGUAGE es-AR es
  [S] :irc.example.net 687 * es-AR es :Se han fijado tus preferencias de idioma.
  [C] NICK pepe
  [C] USER pepe 0 * :Pepe
  [S] :irc.example.net 001 pepe :Bienvenido a la red de IRC example, pepe

LANGUAGE works before and after registration -- before is what lets the
001 come out translated -- and the CAP REQ is not needed: the capability
only advertises.  The replies:

  687 RPL_YOURLANGUAGESARE   <codes> :Language preferences have been set.
                             (already in the language just chosen)
  690 RPL_WHOISLANGUAGE      <nick> <codes> :can speak these languages.
                             (in WHOIS, to the user and to opers)
  981 ERR_TOOMANYLANGUAGES   <max> :You specified too many languages.
  982 ERR_NOLANGUAGE         <codes> :Languages are not supported by this server.
                             (lists only the codes that are not; nothing
                             changes)
  461                        no code given

A code is accepted if some domain has a catalog for it or for its primary
subtag, or if it is "en".  "es-MX" is accepted with only an es.po present.

On the network
--------------

A reply is translated where it is generated, and that is not always the
user's own server: a remote WHOIS, a remote STATS, and every answer from a
service bot that lives on another server.  So the preference travels: a
new P10 token, LG, with the user as source and the same shape as the
command,

  ABAAB LG es-ar es

sent when a registered user runs LANGUAGE, right behind the NICK when a
user who chose before registering is introduced, and in the burst behind
each user that has one (where the AWAY of AWAY_BURST goes; no feature
turns it off).  A server stores what it receives without checking it
against its own catalogs -- a hub with no fr-ca must still pass it on --
and drops, with a protocol violation, only what is not a code at all.
Every server on the network has to run this code, as with +r.

Seeing what is loaded
---------------------

  /STATS n   (or /STATS languages)

lists each domain and language with the entries loaded and rejected at
the last load, the number of distinct preferences interned, the default
and the capability value.  At start-up and on /REHASH the log (LS_CONFIG)
and the opers get one line per rejected entry and per refused file.

  ircd -k

loads the catalogs as well as the configuration and exits with an error if
any file is refused or any entry rejected, so a deployment can check its
translations before restarting.  A broken catalog never stops a normal
start: that language is incomplete or absent, and the server runs.

Limits, all in include/ircd_i18n.h: I18N_LANG_MAX 16 (a code and its
terminator), I18N_PREF_MAX 3 (codes per client), I18N_PREF_TABLE_MAX 4096
(distinct preferences interned; past that a new one is reduced to its first
code, and the log says so once), I18N_NPLURALS_MAX 8, I18N_PO_LINE_MAX
4096.  A client costs two bytes: the preference is an index into a table
of interned preferences, not three strings.


FOR THE PERSON WRITING CODE
===========================

Three macros, in include/ircd_i18n.h, with the names xgettext expects:

  _(to, "text")             translate now, for client `to`
  _n(to, "one", "many", n)  the same, choosing the plural form for n
  N_("text")                mark only; translated later by whoever
                            knows the recipient

_() and _n() return a const char* that lives as long as the catalog: use
it in the same statement, never keep it.

Where the recipient is known and the text goes to one client, mark it:

  sendcmdto_one(&me, CMD_NOTICE, sptr, _(sptr, "%C :Bot %s destroyed"),
                sptr, name);

The whole format is translated, %C and all; the loader guarantees the
directives survive.  send_reply() translates by itself -- a numeric's own
format with the numeric's code as context, an SND_EXPLICIT format with
none -- so an explicit format is only marked:

  send_reply(sptr, SND_EXPLICIT | RPL_REHASHING, N_(":Flushing MOTD cache"));

and the table in s_err.c is written with N(sym, "code", fmt), which
xgettext reads as msgctxt/msgid.

Do not translate the log, anything rendered once for several recipients,
or anything that crosses a link as text (see the list for translators
above).  Do not call any of this from a worker thread: the lookup reads
struct Client.

The cost for a client that asked for nothing, on a server with no
DEFAULT_LANGUAGE, is one comparison and a return.  With a preference it is
at most eight hash probes per message.

In a module
-----------

  #define I18N_DOMAIN mod_i18n
  #include "ircd_i18n.h"
  #include "module.h"

  static struct I18nDomain* mod_i18n;

  static int init(struct ModuleHandle* mod)
  {
    mod_i18n = module_i18n(mod);      /* NULL if no po/ was installed */
    ...
  }

  static void reply(struct Client* bot, struct Client* to, unsigned int n)
  {
    char buf[BUFSIZE];
    ircd_snprintf(NULL, buf, sizeof(buf),
                  _n(to, "You have %u pending request",
                         "You have %u pending requests", n), n);
    bot_send_user(bot, to, 1, buf);
  }

The domain is opened from <module dir>/po/ before mi_init and closed after
mi_fini; /REHASH reloads it with the core's, and /MODULE RELOAD reopens
it.  i18n_text() with a NULL domain returns the original, so a module with
no translations never tests for it.  A module translates with its own
domain before handing text to bot_send_user() or send_reply(); the text it
passes to send_reply() as SND_EXPLICIT is looked up once more in "core",
not found, and left alone.

Text in a table (a command's help line) is marked N_() where it is written
and translated with _() where it is shown.  A module whose own reply
helper translates its format itself has its call sites extracted with a
--keyword of its own in the `pot` target, and they carry no _().

The template for a module is written next to its catalogs by the same
`pot` target: modules/<type>/<name>/po/<name>.pot.


TESTING
=======

  ctest --test-dir build -R ircd_i18n_t

drives the PO reader, the plural evaluator, the format validation and the
lookup chain without a server -- and loads every .po in the source tree,
failing if any entry is rejected.  That is the check that runs in CI, in
place of `msgfmt --check`, which does not understand %C or %Tu (though
`msgfmt -c` still works on the files, because the entries are flagged
no-c-format).  The Docker suite has tests/i18n/.
